in @prism

# @epistemologic/property/monoidal
#
# A type is monoidal iff it carries a binary composition `compose`
# and a distinguished `identity` element such that composition is
# associative and the identity is a two-sided unit:
#
#   identity:        compose(identity, x) == x == compose(x, identity)
#   associativity:   compose(a, compose(b, c)) == compose(compose(a, b), c)
#
# Per Mac Lane (1971) "Categories for the Working Mathematician"
# ch. VII §1: a monoid in a category is a pair (M, ·) with a unit
# element e such that the unit and associativity diagrams commute.
# This property encodes the diagram commutation as a structural check
# on the type's compose / identity surface.
#
# Per #77's parked design call on @fragmentation/frame: the frame
# grammar declared compose + identity actions and noted that the
# pair forms a monoid under content-addressing (hash composition
# is associative; the identity frame is the no-op delta). The
# `requires monoidal(frame)` clause was held until this property
# existed; it can now be applied.
#
# Mirror's contribution: content-addressing makes associativity FREE
# at the substrate. OID concatenation is associative; two frames whose
# (from, to, delta) triples compose to the same end state hash to the
# same OID regardless of the parenthesization of the composition.
# Identity likewise: the empty-delta frame's OID is the canonical
# left/right unit. The verifier reduces to OID equality at every law.
#
# Sibling properties: content_addressed(T) (the substrate condition
# that makes the laws free — without it, associativity would have to
# be proved per-callsite), causality(T) (the partial order monoidal
# composition respects when the carrier is a delta-shape like
# fragmentation.frame).

grammar @epistemologic/property/monoidal {

  # The identity law: there exists an element `e` such that
  # compose(e, x) == x == compose(x, e) for all x of the type.
  # The verifier locates the type's `identity` action, applies
  # it on both sides of compose against an arbitrary x, and
  # checks OID equality with x. Two-sided; failing either side
  # fails the property.
  identity(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The associativity law:
  # compose(a, compose(b, c)) == compose(compose(a, b), c)
  # for all a, b, c of the type. The verifier walks the type's
  # compose action; under content-addressing the law reduces to
  # OID equality between the two parenthesizations.
  associativity(type) -> verdict { \ }

  # The combined property: the type is monoidal.
  # Pass iff identity and associativity both pass. Together these
  # are the monoid axioms (Mac Lane 1971 ch. VII §1).
  monoidal(type) -> verdict { \ }
}

out identity
out associativity
out monoidal
out @epistemologic/property/monoidal
